<DocPage eyebrow="DOCS / GUA 1.1.0" title="Gua 1.1.0 feature reference" lead="Additional typed observations, Trace, semantic regression tools, spatial reads, and bounded input replay extend the existing runtime protocol." description="Verified Gua 1.1.0 additions, package APIs, distribution status, compatibility, and measured support limitations." section="docs">
  <Callout title="Version and scope"><p>This reference covers functionality already in <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/releases/tag/gua-v1.1.0">gua-v1.1.0</a>, source commit <code>968259fbe675bca03cf8c8ef5a3179c7741215a0</code>. On 2026-10-05, Gua main resolves to that same commit: there are no merged changes after the tag. These pages fill gaps in the site's earlier 1.0.7 reference; they do not describe pending PRs as released features.</p></Callout>
  <h2 id="packages">New package entry points</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>gua-value-tools@1.1.0</code></td><td>Common Value types, validation, enum catalogs, serialization, and equality.</td><td><a href="/docs/value/">Value v1 / npm</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Gua.Testing.Snapshots</code> 1.1.0</td><td>Opt-in UI and optional World JSON baselines, typed semantic differences, explicit updates, mask/ignore rules. Targets net10.0 and netstandard2.1.</td><td><a href="/docs/dotnet-packages/">.NET packages</a></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <h2 id="observe">Observe and World Property</h2>
  <p>Register additional typed values explicitly against a published UI/Object owner and retain the registration token for its lifetime. Owner disposal or tree removal invalidates registrations. Host-thread sampling and explicit notification publish changes; the standard World position remains in the World tree. Player-safe values must be allowed by the host, and clients cannot request a Debug elevation.</p>
  <p>Consumers negotiate <code>observe_v1</code> and use <code>get_observe_snapshot</code>, <code>subscribe_observations</code>, <code>poll_observations</code>, and <code>unsubscribe_observations</code>. Subscribe returns an initial snapshot and cursor atomically. Subscriptions belong to one connection or page port; reconnect requires a new subscription. A gap or stale session is missing evidence, not an empty successful observation. Transport results carry the unchanged Observe document and the enum catalogs needed by its values.</p>
  <p>Native, managed, Godot, Unity, Inspector, MCP, and same-page WebMCP entry points are described in the <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/observe-v1.md'}>registration/lifetime contract</a> and <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/observe-transport-v1.md'}>transport contract and engine examples</a>. Web export fixtures use real engine data and a test registration host; they do not establish native browser modelContext or Codex routing coexistence.</p>
  <h2 id="trace">Trace and the offline Viewer</h2>
  <p>Trace v1 saves steps, observed request phases, UI/World/Observe snapshots and changes, assertions, and attachments in a manifest, JSONL events, and content-addressed blobs. Source ID, session epoch, and request ID jointly identify a request. Enqueue acceptance, host completion, state observation, caller outcome, and input cleanup remain distinct facts. Missing intervals, truncated files, retention limits, and capture failures are explicit; Trace never changes the primary test result or replays a scenario.</p>
  <p><code>Gua.Testing</code> embeds the versioned React Viewer, schema bundle, and Observe semantic validator. Inspector's <strong>Open Gua Trace (offline)</strong> panel uses the same Viewer. A package consumer can generate a self-contained report without Node, Bun, a checkout, or network access:</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Generate an offline report" lang="csharp" code={`var report = GuaTraceReport.WriteHtml(trace.ArtifactPath, "artifacts/report.html");
if (!report.Succeeded) Console.WriteLine(report.Error);`} />
  <p>The output must be new; existing artifacts are not overwritten. Screenshot overlays require explicit caller capture and retain their own evidence. Configure sensitive-data handling before capture. Semantic snapshot masking does not redact independent Trace observations or PNGs. See <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/trace-v1.md'}>Trace recording APIs</a>, <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/trace-viewer.md'}>Viewer/report behavior</a>, and <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/trace-integrated-acceptance.md'}>integrated evidence and limitations</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="locators">Locator auto-wait</h2>
  <p><code>GuaAssertions.Query(context)</code> locators resolve the current strict single match when an action is performed. <code>ResolveAsync</code> waits for appearance; <code>WaitForActionableAsync</code> also waits for visibility, enabled state, and the advertised action. Locator actions share one timeout across resolution, actionability, enqueue, and correlated host completion. Multiple matches fail immediately. Rejected enqueue or host failure is not automatically retried, and cancellation after sending does not guarantee rollback. Existing <code>Get()</code> and node expectations retain immediate resolution.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Resolve at action time, then wait for the result state" lang="csharp" code={`var play = GuaAssertions.Query(context).ByRole("button", "Play");
await play.ClickAsync(timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
await GuaAssertions.Query(context).ById("game-screen").ResolveAsync();`} />
  <p>See the <a href={source + 'bindings/dotnet/src/Gua.Testing/README.md'}>locator API and failure contract</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="lint">Semantic lint and Inspector code generation</h2>
  <p><code>GuaSemanticLinter.Analyze</code> and the explicit <code>semantic_lint</code> bridge command analyze committed, projected UI and optional World snapshots. Findings include duplicate IDs, missing parents, cycles, broken World-to-UI references, missing accessible names, and role/action/state contradictions. Lint never runs implicitly during publication, fixes data, or chooses a failure threshold. Stored-snapshot callers must supply already projected trees and their actual profile.</p>
  <p>Inspector can generate C# locators, actions, and assertions for the selected node. Generated source needs review against ID lifetime and test context; the panel does not execute tests. See the <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/semantic-lint-v1.md'}>lint rules and public APIs</a> and <a href={source + 'packages/inspector/src/codegen.ts'}>code generator</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="spatial">Spatial-r1 queries</h2>
  <p>Spatial-r1 adds bounded host physics reads through an explicitly bound, authorized Testing/Debug provider. A Debug observation profile alone is insufficient. Native/managed clients, WebSocket, and native MCP expose provider information and bounded query batches; enqueue acceptance is separate from terminal completion. Reset, provider removal, scene/origin changes, and revocation invalidate old query owners and results. Clients do not implement their own physics or gain arbitrary collision-layer access.</p>
  <p>Built-in privileged browser WebMCP spatial reads are <strong>Unsupported</strong>. World radius searches remain their existing semantic snapshot operation. GodotPhysics3D/Jolt and Unity desktop Mono evidence is scoped to recorded engine versions, backends, recipes, and artifacts. See <a href={source + 'docs/spatial-transport-r1.md'}>operation mapping and support table</a>, <a href={source + 'docs/spatial-engine-r1.md'}>engine provider setup</a>, and <a href={source + 'docs/spatial-acceptance-followup.md'}>packaged route evidence</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="input">InputAction metadata and Timed Segment</h2>
  <p>The capability <code>semantic_game_input_metadata_v1</code> enables explicit <code>get_game_input_actions_v2</code>/<code>find_game_input_actions_v2</code> discovery with optional bounded <code>valueSchema</code> and design-time <code>examples</code>. Legacy v1 commands omit those fields. Examples do not authorize execution or replay; host policy, action-map revision, and confirmation requirements still apply. See the <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/input-action-metadata-v1.md'}>metadata dialect and fallback contract</a>.</p>
  <p>Timed Segment v1 provides explicit finite game-input plans sent at segment-relative offsets, with result polling separate from dispatch and bounded execution/cleanup budgets. Game-input-only Recording v2 import preserves offsets and marks legacy timing provenance as unknown. Mixed UI/input or condition-wait conversion is rejected. Existing MCP/Inspector and sequential Recording replay keep their prior behavior. Current local runtime support is realtime game-input FIFO; simulation, strict application timestamps, and same-tick atomic application require separate host capabilities. A received completion is not a measured host application time. See the <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/timed-segment-v1.md'}>file, scheduler, timing, and cleanup contracts</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="distribution">Distribution and compatibility</h2>
  <p>As of 2026-10-05, four npm and eight NuGet packages are published at 1.1.0. The GitHub Release currently has <strong>no engine archive or Inspector installer assets</strong>. Its asset build failed before packaging because Value was not built before the Vite Inspector build. <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/pull/177">PR #177</a> proposes the workflow correction and is open; its code is outside this tag and does not establish successful release asset publication. Do not replace archive-download pins with 1.1.0 until the required assets are available.</p>
  <p>Package-only consumers can use <code>GuaDistribution.SchemaNames</code>, <code>ReadSchema</code>, <code>ValidateJson</code>, and embedded Viewer metadata/notices. Pin matching package versions, the full source commit, and artifact hashes. JSON Schema validates structure; Trace/Observe semantics and actual engine operations require their own checks. Loading a native library does not prove launch, attachment, or feature execution. See the <a href={source + 'docs/distribution-contract.md'}>distribution APIs and route contract</a>.</p>
  <p>Legacy UI tree v2, selectors, and sequential managed UI Recording v1 remain available. Additional contracts are independently versioned. External validators pinned to strict 1.0.10 schemas must update their schema bundle: Debug diagnostics can add <code>traceLifecycle</code> and authorized spatial version responses can add <code>spatial</code>. Check tightened invalid-input and cancellation edge behavior when upgrading. The <a href={source + 'docs/release-1.1.0.md'}>pre-release compatibility review</a> is historical planning evidence; its proposed/unreleased wording predates registry publication.</p>
  <h2 id="support">Measured support boundaries</h2>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Intel Unity Editor remains unverified"><p>Native Intel macOS, Unity 6000.5.3f1, actual UPM, Editor Play Mode was not executed because no Intel Mac was available. It is <strong>UNVERIFIED</strong>, not failed or Unsupported. Native Apple Silicon Editor and four-RID Mono Player evidence exists within the recorded fixtures and artifacts; it does not prove the Intel Editor route. See <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/issues/174">#174</a> and the <a href={source + 'docs/remaining-route-acceptance.md'}>current acceptance record</a>.</p></Callout>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/issues/116">#116</a> remains open for actual Codex browser/native MCP routing coexistence. Desktop spatial acceptance does not establish WebGL/IL2CPP spatial support or every engine/backend combination. Network mocks and deterministic test starting state remain game/test-fixture responsibilities.</p>
</DocPage>
